During Blackout Periods, directors, executives, and designated employees are prohibited from:

- Purchasing or selling Company Securities;
- Making gifts or charitable donations of Company Securities;
- Making elections, changes, investment decisions, or re-allocations in any 401(k) plan, pension plan, deferred compensation plan, or other benefit or deferral plans that involve Company Securities;
- Effecting cashless exercises of options (except for delivery of shares in payment of tax withholding obligations upon vesting of restricted stock or transactions pursuant to duly adopted Rule 10b5-1 Trading Plans).

These restrictions are enforced to ensure compliance with Insider Trading laws and to prevent unfair advantage from material non-public information (MNPI).

The Blackout Periods generally begin at the close of business on the 15th day of the last month of each fiscal quarter or 30 days prior to the scheduled release of financial results for the quarter (whichever is earlier) and last until the end of the trading day on which financial results are released to the public.

